schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: ActivityPub/Mastodon federates followers but not post content (as of 2025)
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Mastodon's account-migration `Move` activity carries a user's followers
    (and, via exported CSV, their follows) to a new server, but as of 2025
    the actual posts do not move automatically; true content portability is
    still being specified via the W3C Social Web Community Group's
    data-portability draft and the LOLA proposal.
  evidence: >-
    Cited to Mastodon's own migration documentation and the W3C SWICG
    data-portability draft.
  source_lineage: >-
    https://docs.joinmastodon.org/user/moving/ ;
    https://swicg.github.io/activitypub-data-portability/
  interpretation: >-
    Used as the article's "honest caveat" example — showing that even a
    working, widely-adopted federation protocol (ActivityPub) has an
    incomplete portability story, to temper expectations that adopting a
    protocol instantly solves portability too.
  uncertainty: >-
    Standards work (W3C SWICG draft, LOLA) is explicitly in-progress and may
    have moved since the article's writing; status should be re-checked
    before being cited as current.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/federation-portability.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  notes: |
    # assessed-but-excluded
    - "The Order That Saves You" 4-step sequence and the "Try This" prompts —
      operational/coaching guidance, not distinct knowledge objects; captured
      at summary level in the encyclopedia-entry instead of split out.
    - Xebia blog post (Markdown+Git case for docs) — a one-off supporting
      citation, not a living knowledge environment the toolkit will keep
      drawing from; not registered as its own source-system.
